1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:02,832
CHAPTER XLIII.

2
00:00:02,832 --> 00:00:07,200
ELIZABETH, as they drove along, watched for the first appearance of Pemberley Woods with

3
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:11,765
some perturbation; and when at length they turned in at the lodge, her spirits were in

4
00:00:11,765 --> 00:00:13,332
a high flutter.

5
00:00:13,332 --> 00:00:17,832
The park was very large, and contained great variety of ground.

6
00:00:17,832 --> 00:00:22,000
They entered it in one of its lowest points, and drove for some time through a beautiful

7
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:24,832
wood stretching over a wide extent.

8
00:00:24,832 --> 00:00:30,733
Elizabeth’s mind was too full for conversation, but she saw and admired every remarkable spot

9
00:00:30,733 --> 00:00:32,433
and point of view.

10
00:00:32,433 --> 00:00:37,233
They gradually ascended for half a mile, and then found themselves at the top of a considerable

11
00:00:37,233 --> 00:00:42,599
eminence, where the wood ceased, and the eye was instantly caught by Pemberley House, situated

12
00:00:42,599 --> 00:00:47,433
on the opposite side of the valley, into which the road with some abruptness wound.

13
00:00:47,433 --> 00:00:52,900
It was a large, handsome stone building, standing well on rising ground, and backed by a ridge

14
00:00:52,900 --> 00:00:58,265
of high woody hills; and in front a stream of some natural importance was swelled into

15
00:00:58,265 --> 00:01:01,299
greater, but without any artificial appearance.

16
00:01:01,299 --> 00:01:04,532
Its banks were neither formal nor falsely adorned.

17
00:01:04,532 --> 00:01:05,900
Elizabeth was delighted.

18
00:01:05,900 --> 00:01:10,133
She had never seen a place for which nature had done more, or where natural beauty had

19
00:01:10,133 --> 00:01:13,133
been so little counteracted by an awkward taste.

20
00:01:13,133 --> 00:01:18,032
They were all of them warm in their admiration; and at that moment she felt that to be mistress

21
00:01:18,032 --> 00:01:20,332
of Pemberley might be something!

22
00:01:20,332 --> 00:01:24,900
They descended the hill, crossed the bridge, and drove to the door; and, while examining

23
00:01:24,900 --> 00:01:29,633
the nearer aspect of the house, all her apprehension of meeting its owner returned.

24
00:01:29,633 --> 00:01:32,466
She dreaded lest the chambermaid had been mistaken.

25
00:01:32,466 --> 00:01:36,532
On applying to see the place, they were admitted into the hall; and Elizabeth, as they waited

26
00:01:36,532 --> 00:01:40,666
for the housekeeper, had leisure to wonder at her being where she was.

27
00:01:40,666 --> 00:01:45,733
The housekeeper came; a respectable looking elderly woman, much less fine, and more civil,

28
00:01:45,733 --> 00:01:47,933
than she had any notion of finding her.

29
00:01:47,933 --> 00:01:49,966
They followed her into the dining-parlour.

30
00:01:49,966 --> 00:01:53,565
It was a large, well-proportioned room, handsomely fitted up.

31
00:01:53,565 --> 00:01:58,099
Elizabeth, after slightly surveying it, went to a window to enjoy its prospect.

32
00:01:58,099 --> 00:02:02,065
The hill, crowned with wood, from which they had descended, receiving increased abruptness

33
00:02:02,065 --> 00:02:05,233
from the distance, was a beautiful object.

34
00:02:05,233 --> 00:02:09,532
Every disposition of the ground was good; and she looked on the whole scene, the river,

35
00:02:09,532 --> 00:02:13,065
the trees scattered on its banks, and the winding of the valley, as far as she could

36
00:02:13,065 --> 00:02:15,166
trace it, with delight.

37
00:02:15,166 --> 00:02:19,599
As they passed into other rooms, these objects were taking different positions; but from

38
00:02:19,599 --> 00:02:22,265
every window there were beauties to be seen.

39
00:02:22,265 --> 00:02:27,032
The rooms were lofty and handsome, and their furniture suitable to the fortune of their

40
00:02:27,032 --> 00:02:31,400
proprietor; but Elizabeth saw, with admiration of his taste, that it was neither gaudy nor

41
00:02:31,400 --> 00:02:35,933
uselessly fine,—with less of splendour, and more real elegance, than the furniture

42
00:02:35,933 --> 00:02:37,133
of Rosings.

43
00:02:37,133 --> 00:02:42,500
“And of this place,” thought she, “I might have been mistress!

44
00:02:42,500 --> 00:02:46,700
With these rooms I might have now been familiarly acquainted!

45
00:02:46,700 --> 00:02:51,000
Instead of viewing them as a stranger, I might have rejoiced in them as my own, and welcomed

46
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:53,832
to them as visitors my uncle and aunt.

47
00:02:53,832 --> 00:02:59,466
But, no,” recollecting herself, “that could never be; my uncle and aunt would have

48
00:02:59,466 --> 00:03:03,466
been lost to me; I should not have been allowed to invite them.”

49
00:03:03,466 --> 00:03:08,532
This was a lucky recollection—it saved her from something like regret.

50
00:03:08,532 --> 00:03:12,933
She longed to inquire of the housekeeper whether her master were really absent, but had not

51
00:03:12,933 --> 00:03:14,633
courage for it.

52
00:03:14,633 --> 00:03:18,700
At length, however, the question was asked by her uncle; and she turned away with alarm,

53
00:03:18,700 --> 00:03:23,133
while Mrs. Reynolds replied, that he was; adding, “But we expect him to-morrow, with

54
00:03:23,133 --> 00:03:25,332
a large party of friends.”

55
00:03:25,332 --> 00:03:29,233
How rejoiced was Elizabeth that their own journey had not by any circumstance been delayed

56
00:03:29,233 --> 00:03:30,733
a day!

57
00:03:30,733 --> 00:03:33,233
Her aunt now called her to look at a picture.

58
00:03:33,233 --> 00:03:37,532
She approached, and saw the likeness of Mr. Wickham, suspended, amongst several other

59
00:03:37,532 --> 00:03:39,532
miniatures, over the mantel-piece.

60
00:03:39,532 --> 00:03:42,532
Her aunt asked her, smilingly, how she liked it.

61
00:03:42,532 --> 00:03:46,532
The housekeeper came forward, and told them it was the picture of a young gentleman, the

62
00:03:46,532 --> 00:03:50,866
son of her late master’s steward, who had been brought up by him at his own expense.

63
00:03:50,866 --> 00:03:56,700
“He is now gone into the army,” she added; “but I am afraid he has turned out very

64
00:03:56,700 --> 00:03:57,799
wild.”

65
00:03:57,799 --> 00:04:02,000
Mrs. Gardiner looked at her niece with a smile, but Elizabeth could not return it.

66
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:07,933
“And that,” said Mrs. Reynolds, pointing to another of the miniatures, “is my master—and

67
00:04:07,933 --> 00:04:08,966
very like him.

68
00:04:08,966 --> 00:04:12,866
It was drawn at the same time as the other—about eight years ago.”

69
00:04:12,866 --> 00:04:17,500
“I have heard much of your master’s fine person,” said Mrs. Gardiner, looking at

70
00:04:17,500 --> 00:04:19,733
the picture; “it is a handsome face.

71
00:04:19,733 --> 00:04:23,633
But, Lizzy, you can tell us whether it is like or not.”

72
00:04:23,633 --> 00:04:27,466
Mrs. Reynolds’ respect for Elizabeth seemed to increase on this intimation of her knowing

73
00:04:27,466 --> 00:04:28,466
her master.

74
00:04:28,466 --> 00:04:32,233
“Does that young lady know Mr. Darcy?”

75
00:04:32,233 --> 00:04:34,700
Elizabeth coloured, and said, “A little.”

76
00:04:34,700 --> 00:04:38,366
“And do not you think him a very handsome gentleman, ma’am?”

77
00:04:38,366 --> 00:04:40,032
“Yes, very handsome.”

78
00:04:40,032 --> 00:04:45,366
“I am sure I know none so handsome; but in the gallery upstairs you will see a finer,

79
00:04:45,366 --> 00:04:47,133
larger picture of him than this.

80
00:04:47,133 --> 00:04:50,832
This room was my late master’s favourite room, and these miniatures are just as they

81
00:04:50,832 --> 00:04:51,832
used to be then.

82
00:04:51,832 --> 00:04:54,565
He was very fond of them.”

83
00:04:54,565 --> 00:04:58,200
This accounted to Elizabeth for Mr. Wickham’s being among them.

84
00:04:58,200 --> 00:05:02,099
Mrs. Reynolds then directed their attention to one of Miss Darcy, drawn when she was only

85
00:05:02,099 --> 00:05:03,200
eight years old.

86
00:05:03,200 --> 00:05:08,200
“And is Miss Darcy as handsome as her brother?” said Mr. Gardiner.

87
00:05:08,200 --> 00:05:13,565
“Oh, yes—the handsomest young lady that ever was seen; and so accomplished!

88
00:05:13,565 --> 00:05:16,000
She plays and sings all day long.

89
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:20,866
In the next room is a new instrument just come down for her—a present from my master:

90
00:05:20,866 --> 00:05:23,433
she comes here to-morrow with him.”

91
00:05:23,433 --> 00:05:27,500
Mr. Gardiner, whose manners were easy and pleasant, encouraged her communicativeness

92
00:05:27,500 --> 00:05:32,733
by his questions and remarks: Mrs. Reynolds, either from pride or attachment, had evidently

93
00:05:32,733 --> 00:05:35,666
great pleasure in talking of her master and his sister.

94
00:05:35,666 --> 00:05:39,032
“Is your master much at Pemberley in the course of the year?”

95
00:05:39,032 --> 00:05:44,166
“Not so much as I could wish, sir: but I dare say he may spend half his time here;

96
00:05:44,166 --> 00:05:47,000
and Miss Darcy is always down for the summer months.”

97
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:50,832
“Except,” thought Elizabeth, “when she goes to Ramsgate.”

98
00:05:50,832 --> 00:05:54,633
“If your master would marry, you might see more of him.”

99
00:05:54,633 --> 00:05:57,299
“Yes, sir; but I do not know when that will be.

100
00:05:57,299 --> 00:06:00,099
I do not know who is good enough for him.”

101
00:06:00,099 --> 00:06:02,133
Mr. and Mrs. Gardiner smiled.

102
00:06:02,133 --> 00:06:05,733
Elizabeth could not help saying, “It is very much to his credit, I am sure, that you

103
00:06:05,733 --> 00:06:06,733
should think so.”

104
00:06:06,733 --> 00:06:11,633
“I say no more than the truth, and what everybody will say that knows him,” replied

105
00:06:11,633 --> 00:06:12,933
the other.

106
00:06:12,933 --> 00:06:16,500
Elizabeth thought this was going pretty far; and she listened with increasing astonishment

107
00:06:16,500 --> 00:06:20,799
as the housekeeper added, “I have never had a cross word from him in my life, and

108
00:06:20,799 --> 00:06:24,000
I have known him ever since he was four years old.”

109
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:29,299
This was praise of all others most extraordinary, most opposite to her ideas.

110
00:06:29,299 --> 00:06:32,466
That he was not a good-tempered man had been her firmest opinion.

111
00:06:32,466 --> 00:06:37,133
Her keenest attention was awakened: she longed to hear more; and was grateful to her uncle

112
00:06:37,133 --> 00:06:40,065
for saying,— “There are very few people of whom so much

113
00:06:40,065 --> 00:06:41,133
can be said.

114
00:06:41,133 --> 00:06:43,200
You are lucky in having such a master.”

115
00:06:43,200 --> 00:06:45,700
“Yes, sir, I know I am.

116
00:06:45,700 --> 00:06:48,265
If I were to go through the world, I could not meet with a better.

117
00:06:48,265 --> 00:06:52,000
But I have always observed, that they who are good-natured when children, are good-natured

118
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:56,900
when they grow up; and he was always the sweetest tempered, most generous-hearted boy in the

119
00:06:56,900 --> 00:06:58,500
world.”

120
00:06:58,500 --> 00:06:59,966
Elizabeth almost stared at her.

121
00:06:59,966 --> 00:07:01,900
“Can this be Mr. Darcy?”

122
00:07:01,900 --> 00:07:03,200
thought she.

123
00:07:03,200 --> 00:07:06,565
“His father was an excellent man,” said Mrs. Gardiner.

124
00:07:06,565 --> 00:07:12,765
“Yes, ma’am, that he was indeed; and his son will be just like him—just as affable

125
00:07:12,765 --> 00:07:14,466
to the poor.”

126
00:07:14,466 --> 00:07:18,633
Elizabeth listened, wondered, doubted, and was impatient for more.

127
00:07:18,633 --> 00:07:21,433
Mrs. Reynolds could interest her on no other point.

128
00:07:21,433 --> 00:07:25,133
She related the subjects of the pictures, the dimensions of the rooms, and the price

129
00:07:25,133 --> 00:07:27,133
of the furniture in vain.

130
00:07:27,133 --> 00:07:30,799
Mr. Gardiner, highly amused by the kind of family prejudice, to which he attributed her

131
00:07:30,799 --> 00:07:35,532
excessive commendation of her master, soon led again to the subject; and she dwelt with

132
00:07:35,532 --> 00:07:39,799
energy on his many merits, as they proceeded together up the great staircase.

133
00:07:39,799 --> 00:07:45,832
“He is the best landlord, and the best master,” said she, “that ever lived.

134
00:07:45,832 --> 00:07:49,366
Not like the wild young men now-a-days, who think of nothing but themselves.

135
00:07:49,366 --> 00:07:52,565
There is not one of his tenants or servants but what will give him a good name.

136
00:07:52,565 --> 00:07:56,433
Some people call him proud; but I am sure I never saw anything of it.

137
00:07:56,433 --> 00:08:00,032
To my fancy, it is only because he does not rattle away like other young men.”

138
00:08:00,032 --> 00:08:02,832
“In what an amiable light does this place him!”

139
00:08:02,832 --> 00:08:03,933
thought Elizabeth.

140
00:08:03,933 --> 00:08:09,500
“This fine account of him,” whispered her aunt as they walked, “is not quite consistent

141
00:08:09,500 --> 00:08:12,065
with his behaviour to our poor friend.”

142
00:08:12,065 --> 00:08:14,400
“Perhaps we might be deceived.”

143
00:08:14,400 --> 00:08:18,966
“That is not very likely; our authority was too good.”

144
00:08:18,966 --> 00:08:22,966
On reaching the spacious lobby above, they were shown into a very pretty sitting-room,

145
00:08:22,966 --> 00:08:26,966
lately fitted up with greater elegance and lightness than the apartments below; and were

146
00:08:26,966 --> 00:08:31,166
informed that it was but just done to give pleasure to Miss Darcy, who had taken a liking

147
00:08:31,166 --> 00:08:32,633
to the room, when last at Pemberley.

148
00:08:32,633 --> 00:08:37,532
“He is certainly a good brother,” said Elizabeth, as she walked towards one of the

149
00:08:37,532 --> 00:08:38,700
windows.

150
00:08:38,700 --> 00:08:42,500
Mrs. Reynolds anticipated Miss Darcy’s delight, when she should enter the room.

151
00:08:42,500 --> 00:08:46,133
“And this is always the way with him,” she added.

152
00:08:46,133 --> 00:08:49,700
“Whatever can give his sister any pleasure, is sure to be done in a moment.

153
00:08:49,700 --> 00:08:52,433
There is nothing he would not do for her.”

154
00:08:52,433 --> 00:08:56,299
The picture gallery, and two or three of the principal bed-rooms, were all that remained

155
00:08:56,299 --> 00:08:57,433
to be shown.

156
00:08:57,433 --> 00:09:01,466
In the former were many good paintings: but Elizabeth knew nothing of the art; and from

157
00:09:01,466 --> 00:09:05,065
such as had been already visible below, she had willingly turned to look at some drawings

158
00:09:05,065 --> 00:09:10,200
of Miss Darcy’s, in crayons, whose subjects were usually more interesting, and also more

159
00:09:10,200 --> 00:09:11,233
intelligible.

160
00:09:11,233 --> 00:09:15,000
In the gallery there were many family portraits, but they could have little to fix the attention

161
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:16,599
of a stranger.

162
00:09:16,599 --> 00:09:20,299
Elizabeth walked on in quest of the only face whose features would be known to her.

163
00:09:20,299 --> 00:09:25,332
At last it arrested her—and she beheld a striking resemblance of Mr. Darcy, with such

164
00:09:25,332 --> 00:09:29,265
a smile over the face, as she remembered to have sometimes seen, when he looked at her.

165
00:09:29,265 --> 00:09:33,366
She stood several minutes before the picture, in earnest contemplation, and returned to

166
00:09:33,366 --> 00:09:35,832
it again before they quitted the gallery.

167
00:09:35,832 --> 00:09:40,599
Mrs. Reynolds informed them, that it had been taken in his father’s lifetime.

168
00:09:40,599 --> 00:09:44,633
There was certainly at this moment, in Elizabeth’s mind, a more gentle sensation towards the

169
00:09:44,633 --> 00:09:47,733
original than she had ever felt in the height of their acquaintance.

170
00:09:47,733 --> 00:09:51,832
The commendation bestowed on him by Mrs. Reynolds was of no trifling nature.

171
00:09:51,832 --> 00:09:55,233
What praise is more valuable than the praise of an intelligent servant?

172
00:09:55,233 --> 00:10:00,233
As a brother, a landlord, a master, she considered how many people’s happiness were in his

173
00:10:00,233 --> 00:10:01,233
guardianship!

174
00:10:01,233 --> 00:10:04,265
How much of pleasure or pain it was in his power to bestow!

175
00:10:04,265 --> 00:10:07,299
How much of good or evil must be done by him!

176
00:10:07,299 --> 00:10:11,633
Every idea that had been brought forward by the housekeeper was favourable to his character;

177
00:10:11,633 --> 00:10:15,599
and as she stood before the canvas, on which he was represented, and fixed his eyes upon

178
00:10:15,599 --> 00:10:19,466
herself, she thought of his regard with a deeper sentiment of gratitude than it had

179
00:10:19,466 --> 00:10:25,332
ever raised before: she remembered its warmth, and softened its impropriety of expression.

180
00:10:25,332 --> 00:10:29,400
When all of the house that was open to general inspection had been seen, they returned down

181
00:10:29,400 --> 00:10:34,133
stairs; and, taking leave of the housekeeper, were consigned over to the gardener, who met

182
00:10:34,133 --> 00:10:35,933
them at the hall door.

183
00:10:35,933 --> 00:10:40,532
As they walked across the lawn towards the river, Elizabeth turned back to look again;

184
00:10:40,532 --> 00:10:44,666
her uncle and aunt stopped also; and while the former was conjecturing as to the date

185
00:10:44,666 --> 00:10:49,433
of the building, the owner of it himself suddenly came forward from the road which led behind

186
00:10:49,433 --> 00:10:51,233
it to the stables.

187
00:10:51,233 --> 00:10:55,633
They were within twenty yards of each other; and so abrupt was his appearance, that it

188
00:10:55,633 --> 00:10:57,866
was impossible to avoid his sight.

189
00:10:57,866 --> 00:11:02,733
Their eyes instantly met, and the cheeks of each were overspread with the deepest blush.

190
00:11:02,733 --> 00:11:08,200
He absolutely started, and for a moment seemed immovable from surprise; but shortly recovering

191
00:11:08,200 --> 00:11:14,000
himself, advanced towards the party, and spoke to Elizabeth, if not in terms of perfect composure,

192
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:16,400
at least of perfect civility.

193
00:11:16,400 --> 00:11:21,000
She had instinctively turned away; but stopping on his approach, received his compliments

194
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:24,065
with an embarrassment impossible to be overcome.

195
00:11:24,065 --> 00:11:27,765
Had his first appearance, or his resemblance to the picture they had just been examining,

196
00:11:27,765 --> 00:11:32,133
been insufficient to assure the other two that they now saw Mr. Darcy, the gardener’s

197
00:11:32,133 --> 00:11:35,933
expression of surprise, on beholding his master, must immediately have told it.

198
00:11:35,933 --> 00:11:40,366
They stood a little aloof while he was talking to their niece, who, astonished and confused,

199
00:11:40,366 --> 00:11:44,799
scarcely dared lift her eyes to his face, and knew not what answer she returned to his

200
00:11:44,799 --> 00:11:47,099
civil inquiries after her family.

201
00:11:47,099 --> 00:11:51,265
Amazed at the alteration of his manner since they last parted, every sentence that he uttered

202
00:11:51,265 --> 00:11:55,733
was increasing her embarrassment; and every idea of the impropriety of her being found

203
00:11:55,733 --> 00:11:59,200
there recurring to her mind, the few minutes in which they continued together were some

204
00:11:59,200 --> 00:12:01,332
of the most uncomfortable of her life.

205
00:12:01,332 --> 00:12:06,866
Nor did he seem much more at ease; when he spoke, his accent had none of its usual sedateness;

206
00:12:06,866 --> 00:12:11,032
and he repeated his inquiries as to the time of her having left Longbourn, and of her stay

207
00:12:11,032 --> 00:12:16,166
in Derbyshire, so often, and in so hurried a way, as plainly spoke the distraction of

208
00:12:16,166 --> 00:12:17,599
his thoughts.

209
00:12:17,599 --> 00:12:21,400
At length, every idea seemed to fail him; and after standing a few moments without saying

210
00:12:21,400 --> 00:12:25,500
a word, he suddenly recollected himself, and took leave.

211
00:12:25,500 --> 00:12:29,599
The others then joined her, and expressed their admiration of his figure; but Elizabeth

212
00:12:29,599 --> 00:12:34,532
heard not a word, and, wholly engrossed by her own feelings, followed them in silence.

213
00:12:34,532 --> 00:12:37,565
She was overpowered by shame and vexation.

214
00:12:37,565 --> 00:12:42,032
Her coming there was the most unfortunate, the most ill-judged thing in the world!

215
00:12:42,032 --> 00:12:43,666
How strange must it appear to him!

216
00:12:43,666 --> 00:12:47,466
In what a disgraceful light might it not strike so vain a man!

217
00:12:47,466 --> 00:12:50,866
It might seem as if she had purposely thrown herself in his way again!

218
00:12:50,866 --> 00:12:51,866
Oh!

219
00:12:51,866 --> 00:12:56,099
why did she come? or, why did he thus come a day before he was expected?

220
00:12:56,099 --> 00:13:00,200
Had they been only ten minutes sooner, they should have been beyond the reach of his discrimination;

221
00:13:00,200 --> 00:13:04,765
for it was plain that he was that moment arrived, that moment alighted from his horse or his

222
00:13:04,765 --> 00:13:05,765
carriage.

223
00:13:05,765 --> 00:13:09,166
She blushed again and again over the perverseness of the meeting.

224
00:13:09,166 --> 00:13:13,633
And his behaviour, so strikingly altered,—what could it mean?

225
00:13:13,633 --> 00:13:18,400
That he should even speak to her was amazing!—but to speak with such civility, to inquire after

226
00:13:18,400 --> 00:13:19,900
her family!

227
00:13:19,900 --> 00:13:23,733
Never in her life had she seen his manners so little dignified, never had he spoken with

228
00:13:23,733 --> 00:13:26,966
such gentleness as on this unexpected meeting.

229
00:13:26,966 --> 00:13:31,532
What a contrast did it offer to his last address in Rosings Park, when he put his letter into

230
00:13:31,532 --> 00:13:32,599
her hand!

231
00:13:32,599 --> 00:13:36,032
She knew not what to think, or how to account for it.

232
00:13:36,032 --> 00:13:40,065
They had now entered a beautiful walk by the side of the water, and every step was bringing

233
00:13:40,065 --> 00:13:44,400
forward a nobler fall of ground, or a finer reach of the woods to which they were approaching:

234
00:13:44,400 --> 00:13:48,933
but it was some time before Elizabeth was sensible of any of it; and, though she answered

235
00:13:48,933 --> 00:13:53,000
mechanically to the repeated appeals of her uncle and aunt, and seemed to direct her eyes

236
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:57,332
to such objects as they pointed out, she distinguished no part of the scene.

237
00:13:57,332 --> 00:14:02,000
Her thoughts were all fixed on that one spot of Pemberley House, whichever it might be,

238
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:04,233
where Mr. Darcy then was.

239
00:14:04,233 --> 00:14:08,032
She longed to know what at that moment was passing in his mind; in what manner he thought

240
00:14:08,032 --> 00:14:13,466
of her, and whether, in defiance of everything, she was still dear to him.

241
00:14:13,466 --> 00:14:17,500
Perhaps he had been civil only because he felt himself at ease; yet there had been that

242
00:14:17,500 --> 00:14:19,332
in his voice, which was not like ease.

243
00:14:19,332 --> 00:14:23,866
Whether he had felt more of pain or of pleasure in seeing her, she could not tell, but he

244
00:14:23,866 --> 00:14:26,532
certainly had not seen her with composure.

245
00:14:26,532 --> 00:14:30,832
At length, however, the remarks of her companions on her absence of mind roused her, and she

246
00:14:30,832 --> 00:14:33,799
felt the necessity of appearing more like herself.

247
00:14:33,799 --> 00:14:37,500
They entered the woods, and, bidding adieu to the river for a while, ascended some of

248
00:14:37,500 --> 00:14:41,466
the higher grounds; whence, in spots where the opening of the trees gave the eye power

249
00:14:41,466 --> 00:14:45,900
to wander, were many charming views of the valley, the opposite hills, with the long

250
00:14:45,900 --> 00:14:50,466
range of woods overspreading many, and occasionally part of the stream.

251
00:14:50,466 --> 00:14:54,733
Mr. Gardiner expressed a wish of going round the whole park, but feared it might be beyond

252
00:14:54,733 --> 00:14:55,933
a walk.

253
00:14:55,933 --> 00:14:59,366
With a triumphant smile, they were told, that it was ten miles round.

254
00:14:59,366 --> 00:15:03,599
It settled the matter; and they pursued the accustomed circuit; which brought them again,

255
00:15:03,599 --> 00:15:07,700
after some time, in a descent among hanging woods, to the edge of the water, and one of

256
00:15:07,700 --> 00:15:09,433
its narrowest parts.

257
00:15:09,433 --> 00:15:14,133
They crossed it by a simple bridge, in character with the general air of the scene: it was

258
00:15:14,133 --> 00:15:19,332
a spot less adorned than any they had yet visited; and the valley, here contracted into

259
00:15:19,332 --> 00:15:23,933
a glen, allowed room only for the stream, and a narrow walk amidst the rough coppice-wood

260
00:15:23,933 --> 00:15:25,733
which bordered it.

261
00:15:25,733 --> 00:15:29,832
Elizabeth longed to explore its windings; but when they had crossed the bridge, and

262
00:15:29,832 --> 00:15:34,133
perceived their distance from the house, Mrs. Gardiner, who was not a great walker, could

263
00:15:34,133 --> 00:15:38,700
go no farther, and thought only of returning to the carriage as quickly as possible.

264
00:15:38,700 --> 00:15:42,599
Her niece was, therefore, obliged to submit, and they took their way towards the house

265
00:15:42,599 --> 00:15:47,000
on the opposite side of the river, in the nearest direction; but their progress was

266
00:15:47,000 --> 00:15:52,000
slow, for Mr. Gardiner, though seldom able to indulge the taste, was very fond of fishing,

267
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:56,832
and was so much engaged in watching the occasional appearance of some trout in the water, and

268
00:15:56,832 --> 00:16:00,332
talking to the man about them, that he advanced but little.

269
00:16:00,332 --> 00:16:04,700
Whilst wandering on in this slow manner, they were again surprised, and Elizabeth’s astonishment

270
00:16:04,700 --> 00:16:08,933
was quite equal to what it had been at first, by the sight of Mr. Darcy approaching them,

271
00:16:08,933 --> 00:16:10,765
and at no great distance.

272
00:16:10,765 --> 00:16:15,265
The walk being here less sheltered than on the other side, allowed them to see him before

273
00:16:15,265 --> 00:16:16,265
they met.

274
00:16:16,265 --> 00:16:20,733
Elizabeth, however astonished, was at least more prepared for an interview than before,

275
00:16:20,733 --> 00:16:25,500
and resolved to appear and to speak with calmness, if he really intended to meet them.

276
00:16:25,500 --> 00:16:29,233
For a few moments, indeed, she felt that he would probably strike into some other path.

277
00:16:29,233 --> 00:16:33,466
The idea lasted while a turning in the walk concealed him from their view; the turning

278
00:16:33,466 --> 00:16:35,666
past, he was immediately before them.

279
00:16:35,666 --> 00:16:40,532
With a glance she saw that he had lost none of his recent civility; and, to imitate his

280
00:16:40,532 --> 00:16:45,032
politeness, she began as they met to admire the beauty of the place; but she had not got

281
00:16:45,032 --> 00:16:50,166
beyond the words “delightful,” and “charming,” when some unlucky recollections obtruded,

282
00:16:50,166 --> 00:16:54,500
and she fancied that praise of Pemberley from her might be mischievously construed.

283
00:16:54,500 --> 00:16:57,065
Her colour changed, and she said no more.

284
00:16:57,065 --> 00:17:01,565
Mrs. Gardiner was standing a little behind; and on her pausing, he asked her if she would

285
00:17:01,565 --> 00:17:04,500
do him the honour of introducing him to her friends.

286
00:17:04,500 --> 00:17:08,265
This was a stroke of civility for which she was quite unprepared; and she could hardly

287
00:17:08,265 --> 00:17:12,799
suppress a smile at his being now seeking the acquaintance of some of those very people,

288
00:17:12,799 --> 00:17:16,032
against whom his pride had revolted, in his offer to herself.

289
00:17:16,032 --> 00:17:21,000
“What will be his surprise,” thought she, “when he knows who they are!

290
00:17:21,000 --> 00:17:23,733
He takes them now for people of fashion.”

291
00:17:23,733 --> 00:17:27,433
The introduction, however, was immediately made; and as she named their relationship

292
00:17:27,433 --> 00:17:31,933
to herself, she stole a sly look at him, to see how he bore it; and was not without the

293
00:17:31,933 --> 00:17:36,866
expectation of his decamping as fast as he could from such disgraceful companions.

294
00:17:36,866 --> 00:17:41,200
That he was surprised by the connection was evident: he sustained it, however, with fortitude:

295
00:17:41,200 --> 00:17:45,466
and, so far from going away, turned back with them, and entered into conversation with Mr.

296
00:17:45,466 --> 00:17:46,799
Gardiner.

297
00:17:46,799 --> 00:17:50,233
Elizabeth could not but be pleased, could not but triumph.

298
00:17:50,233 --> 00:17:53,900
It was consoling that he should know she had some relations for whom there was no need

299
00:17:53,900 --> 00:17:55,065
to blush.

300
00:17:55,065 --> 00:17:59,466
She listened most attentively to all that passed between them, and gloried in every

301
00:17:59,466 --> 00:18:04,000
expression, every sentence of her uncle, which marked his intelligence, his taste, or his

302
00:18:04,000 --> 00:18:05,466
good manners.

303
00:18:05,466 --> 00:18:09,633
The conversation soon turned upon fishing; and she heard Mr. Darcy invite him, with the

304
00:18:09,633 --> 00:18:14,400
greatest civility, to fish there as often as he chose, while he continued in the neighbourhood,

305
00:18:14,400 --> 00:18:18,166
offering at the same time to supply him with fishing tackle, and pointing out those parts

306
00:18:18,166 --> 00:18:20,933
of the stream where there was usually most sport.

307
00:18:20,933 --> 00:18:24,866
Mrs. Gardiner, who was walking arm in arm with Elizabeth, gave her a look expressive

308
00:18:24,866 --> 00:18:26,500
of her wonder.

309
00:18:26,500 --> 00:18:30,866
Elizabeth said nothing, but it gratified her exceedingly; the compliment must be all for

310
00:18:30,866 --> 00:18:31,866
herself.

311
00:18:31,866 --> 00:18:36,700
Her astonishment, however, was extreme; and continually was she repeating, “Why is he

312
00:18:36,700 --> 00:18:37,700
so altered?

313
00:18:37,700 --> 00:18:39,400
From what can it proceed?

314
00:18:39,400 --> 00:18:44,200
It cannot be for me, it cannot be for my sake that his manners are thus softened.

315
00:18:44,200 --> 00:18:47,733
My reproofs at Hunsford could not work such a change as this.

316
00:18:47,733 --> 00:18:50,433
It is impossible that he should still love me.”

317
00:18:50,433 --> 00:18:55,133
After walking some time in this way, the two ladies in front, the two gentlemen behind,

318
00:18:55,133 --> 00:18:59,000
on resuming their places, after descending to the brink of the river for the better inspection

319
00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:03,299
of some curious water-plant, there chanced to be a little alteration.

320
00:19:03,299 --> 00:19:08,366
It originated in Mrs. Gardiner, who, fatigued by the exercise of the morning, found Elizabeth’s

321
00:19:08,366 --> 00:19:12,565
arm inadequate to her support, and consequently preferred her husband’s.

322
00:19:12,565 --> 00:19:16,765
Mr. Darcy took her place by her niece, and they walked on together.

323
00:19:16,765 --> 00:19:19,466
After a short silence the lady first spoke.

324
00:19:19,466 --> 00:19:23,500
She wished him to know that she had been assured of his absence before she came to the place,

325
00:19:23,500 --> 00:19:27,265
and accordingly began by observing, that his arrival had been very unexpected—“for

326
00:19:27,265 --> 00:19:32,900
your housekeeper,” she added, “informed us that you would certainly not be here till

327
00:19:32,900 --> 00:19:36,733
to-morrow; and, indeed, before we left Bakewell, we understood that you were not immediately

328
00:19:36,733 --> 00:19:38,900
expected in the country.”

329
00:19:38,900 --> 00:19:43,032
He acknowledged the truth of it all; and said that business with his steward had occasioned

330
00:19:43,032 --> 00:19:47,200
his coming forward a few hours before the rest of the party with whom he had been travelling.

331
00:19:47,200 --> 00:19:51,299
“They will join me early to-morrow,” he continued, “and among them are some who

332
00:19:51,299 --> 00:19:55,433
will claim an acquaintance with you,—Mr. Bingley and his sisters.”

333
00:19:55,433 --> 00:19:57,500
Elizabeth answered only by a slight bow.

334
00:19:57,500 --> 00:20:01,200
Her thoughts were instantly driven back to the time when Mr. Bingley’s name had been

335
00:20:01,200 --> 00:20:06,099
last mentioned between them; and if she might judge from his complexion, his mind was not

336
00:20:06,099 --> 00:20:07,633
very differently engaged.

337
00:20:07,633 --> 00:20:13,500
“There is also one other person in the party,” he continued after a pause, “who more particularly

338
00:20:13,500 --> 00:20:15,700
wishes to be known to you.

339
00:20:15,700 --> 00:20:20,700
Will you allow me, or do I ask too much, to introduce my sister to your acquaintance during

340
00:20:20,700 --> 00:20:22,332
your stay at Lambton?”

341
00:20:22,332 --> 00:20:27,265
The surprise of such an application was great indeed; it was too great for her to know in

342
00:20:27,265 --> 00:20:29,332
what manner she acceded to it.

343
00:20:29,332 --> 00:20:32,366
She immediately felt that whatever desire Miss Darcy might have of being acquainted

344
00:20:32,366 --> 00:20:37,233
with her, must be the work of her brother, and without looking farther, it was satisfactory;

345
00:20:37,233 --> 00:20:42,032
it was gratifying to know that his resentment had not made him think really ill of her.

346
00:20:42,032 --> 00:20:46,032
They now walked on in silence; each of them deep in thought.

347
00:20:46,032 --> 00:20:50,700
Elizabeth was not comfortable; that was impossible; but she was flattered and pleased.

348
00:20:50,700 --> 00:20:54,866
His wish of introducing his sister to her was a compliment of the highest kind.

349
00:20:54,866 --> 00:20:58,900
They soon outstripped the others; and when they had reached the carriage, Mr. and Mrs.

350
00:20:58,900 --> 00:21:01,700
Gardiner were half a quarter of a mile behind.

351
00:21:01,700 --> 00:21:06,065
He then asked her to walk into the house—but she declared herself not tired, and they stood

352
00:21:06,065 --> 00:21:07,599
together on the lawn.

353
00:21:07,599 --> 00:21:11,900
At such a time much might have been said, and silence was very awkward.

354
00:21:11,900 --> 00:21:15,866
She wanted to talk, but there seemed an embargo on every subject.

355
00:21:15,866 --> 00:21:19,832
At last she recollected that she had been travelling, and they talked of Matlock and

356
00:21:19,832 --> 00:21:22,265
Dovedale with great perseverance.

357
00:21:22,265 --> 00:21:27,200
Yet time and her aunt moved slowly—and her patience and her ideas were nearly worn out

358
00:21:27,200 --> 00:21:29,200
before the tête-à-tête was over.

359
00:21:29,200 --> 00:21:32,933
On Mr. and Mrs. Gardiner’s coming up they were all pressed to go into the house and

360
00:21:32,933 --> 00:21:38,933
take some refreshment; but this was declined, and they parted on each side with the utmost

361
00:21:38,933 --> 00:21:39,933
politeness.

362
00:21:39,933 --> 00:21:43,500
Mr. Darcy handed the ladies into the carriage; and when it drove off, Elizabeth saw him walking

363
00:21:43,500 --> 00:21:46,133
slowly towards the house.

364
00:21:46,133 --> 00:21:50,065
The observations of her uncle and aunt now began; and each of them pronounced him to

365
00:21:50,065 --> 00:21:52,700
be infinitely superior to anything they had expected.

366
00:21:52,700 --> 00:21:58,065
“He is perfectly well-behaved, polite, and unassuming,” said her uncle.

367
00:21:58,065 --> 00:22:02,565
“There is something a little stately in him, to be sure,” replied her aunt; “but

368
00:22:02,565 --> 00:22:06,265
it is confined to his air, and is not unbecoming.

369
00:22:06,265 --> 00:22:10,633
I can now say with the housekeeper, that though some people may call him proud, I have seen

370
00:22:10,633 --> 00:22:12,065
nothing of it.”

371
00:22:12,065 --> 00:22:15,166
“I was never more surprised than by his behaviour to us.

372
00:22:15,166 --> 00:22:20,265
It was more than civil; it was really attentive; and there was no necessity for such attention.

373
00:22:20,265 --> 00:22:22,733
His acquaintance with Elizabeth was very trifling.”

374
00:22:22,733 --> 00:22:28,065
“To be sure, Lizzy,” said her aunt, “he is not so handsome as Wickham; or rather he

375
00:22:28,065 --> 00:22:31,832
has not Wickham’s countenance, for his features are perfectly good.

376
00:22:31,832 --> 00:22:35,332
But how came you to tell us that he was so disagreeable?”

377
00:22:35,332 --> 00:22:38,799
Elizabeth excused herself as well as she could: said that she had liked him better when they

378
00:22:38,799 --> 00:22:43,265
met in Kent than before, and that she had never seen him so pleasant as this morning.

379
00:22:43,265 --> 00:22:47,900
“But perhaps he may be a little whimsical in his civilities,” replied her uncle.

380
00:22:47,900 --> 00:22:51,233
“Your great men often are; and therefore I shall not take him at his word about fishing,

381
00:22:51,233 --> 00:22:54,900
as he might change his mind another day, and warn me off his grounds.”

382
00:22:54,900 --> 00:22:58,799
Elizabeth felt that they had entirely mistaken his character, but said nothing.

383
00:22:58,799 --> 00:23:03,166
“From what we have seen of him,” continued Mrs. Gardiner, “I really should not have

384
00:23:03,166 --> 00:23:07,366
thought that he could have behaved in so cruel a way by anybody as he has done by poor Wickham.

385
00:23:07,366 --> 00:23:09,633
He has not an ill-natured look.

386
00:23:09,633 --> 00:23:13,233
On the contrary, there is something pleasing about his mouth when he speaks.

387
00:23:13,233 --> 00:23:16,866
And there is something of dignity in his countenance, that would not give one an unfavourable idea

388
00:23:16,866 --> 00:23:17,866
of his heart.

389
00:23:17,866 --> 00:23:22,700
But, to be sure, the good lady who showed us the house did give him a most flaming character!

390
00:23:22,700 --> 00:23:25,032
I could hardly help laughing aloud sometimes.

391
00:23:25,032 --> 00:23:29,433
But he is a liberal master, I suppose, and that, in the eye of a servant, comprehends

392
00:23:29,433 --> 00:23:32,099
every virtue.”

393
00:23:32,099 --> 00:23:35,500
Elizabeth here felt herself called on to say something in vindication of his behaviour

394
00:23:35,500 --> 00:23:40,299
to Wickham; and, therefore, gave them to understand, in as guarded a manner as she could, that

395
00:23:40,299 --> 00:23:44,599
by what she had heard from his relations in Kent, his actions were capable of a very different

396
00:23:44,599 --> 00:23:49,633
construction; and that his character was by no means so faulty, nor Wickham’s so amiable,

397
00:23:49,633 --> 00:23:51,933
as they had been considered in Hertfordshire.

398
00:23:51,933 --> 00:23:56,332
In confirmation of this, she related the particulars of all the pecuniary transactions in which

399
00:23:56,332 --> 00:24:00,500
they had been connected, without actually naming her authority, but stating it to be

400
00:24:00,500 --> 00:24:02,666
such as might be relied on.

401
00:24:02,666 --> 00:24:06,500
Mrs. Gardiner was surprised and concerned: but as they were now approaching the scene

402
00:24:06,500 --> 00:24:11,200
of her former pleasures, every idea gave way to the charm of recollection; and she was

403
00:24:11,200 --> 00:24:15,332
too much engaged in pointing out to her husband all the interesting spots in its environs,

404
00:24:15,332 --> 00:24:16,532
to think of anything else.

405
00:24:16,532 --> 00:24:20,733
Fatigued as she had been by the morning’s walk, they had no sooner dined than she set

406
00:24:20,733 --> 00:24:24,799
off again in quest of her former acquaintance, and the evening was spent in the satisfactions

407
00:24:24,799 --> 00:24:27,466
of an intercourse renewed after many years’ discontinuance.

408
00:24:27,466 --> 00:24:32,000
The occurrences of the day were too full of interest to leave Elizabeth much attention

409
00:24:32,000 --> 00:24:36,733
for any of these new friends; and she could do nothing but think, and think with wonder,

410
00:24:36,733 --> 00:24:41,900
of Mr. Darcy’s civility, and, above all, of his wishing her to be acquainted with his

411
00:24:41,900 --> 00:24:51,265
sister.

412
00:24:51,265 --> 00:24:54,332
CHAPTER XLIV.

413
00:24:54,332 --> 00:24:58,332
ELIZABETH had settled it that Mr. Darcy would bring his sister to visit her the very day

414
00:24:58,332 --> 00:25:03,133
after her reaching Pemberley; and was, consequently, resolved not to be out of sight of the inn

415
00:25:03,133 --> 00:25:05,133
the whole of that morning.

416
00:25:05,133 --> 00:25:09,966
But her conclusion was false; for on the very morning after their own arrival at Lambton

417
00:25:09,966 --> 00:25:11,565
these visitors came.

418
00:25:11,565 --> 00:25:15,299
They had been walking about the place with some of their new friends, and were just returned

419
00:25:15,299 --> 00:25:19,233
to the inn to dress themselves for dining with the same family, when the sound of a

420
00:25:19,233 --> 00:25:23,933
carriage drew them to a window, and they saw a gentleman and lady in a curricle driving

421
00:25:23,933 --> 00:25:25,433
up the street.

422
00:25:25,433 --> 00:25:30,233
Elizabeth, immediately recognizing the livery, guessed what it meant, and imparted no small

423
00:25:30,233 --> 00:25:35,733
degree of surprise to her relations, by acquainting them with the honour which she expected.

424
00:25:35,733 --> 00:25:40,332
Her uncle and aunt were all amazement; and the embarrassment of her manner as she spoke,

425
00:25:40,332 --> 00:25:45,500
joined to the circumstance itself, and many of the circumstances of the preceding day,

426
00:25:45,500 --> 00:25:48,532
opened to them a new idea on the business.

427
00:25:48,532 --> 00:25:52,032
Nothing had ever suggested it before, but they now felt that there was no other way

428
00:25:52,032 --> 00:25:56,765
of accounting for such attentions from such a quarter than by supposing a partiality for

429
00:25:56,765 --> 00:25:58,133
their niece.

430
00:25:58,133 --> 00:26:02,733
While these newly-born notions were passing in their heads, the perturbation of Elizabeth’s

431
00:26:02,733 --> 00:26:05,133
feelings was every moment increasing.

432
00:26:05,133 --> 00:26:10,433
She was quite amazed at her own discomposure; but, amongst other causes of disquiet, she

433
00:26:10,433 --> 00:26:15,099
dreaded lest the partiality of the brother should have said too much in her favour; and,

434
00:26:15,099 --> 00:26:19,966
more than commonly anxious to please, she naturally suspected that every power of pleasing

435
00:26:19,966 --> 00:26:21,332
would fail her.

436
00:26:21,332 --> 00:26:25,799
She retreated from the window, fearful of being seen; and as she walked up and down

437
00:26:25,799 --> 00:26:30,666
the room, endeavouring to compose herself, saw such looks of inquiring surprise in her

438
00:26:30,666 --> 00:26:33,765
uncle and aunt as made everything worse.

439
00:26:33,765 --> 00:26:38,966
Miss Darcy and her brother appeared, and this formidable introduction took place.

440
00:26:38,966 --> 00:26:43,099
With astonishment did Elizabeth see that her new acquaintance was at least as much embarrassed

441
00:26:43,099 --> 00:26:44,666
as herself.

442
00:26:44,666 --> 00:26:49,166
Since her being at Lambton, she had heard that Miss Darcy was exceedingly proud; but

443
00:26:49,166 --> 00:26:53,799
the observation of a very few minutes convinced her that she was only exceedingly shy.

444
00:26:53,799 --> 00:26:59,233
She found it difficult to obtain even a word from her beyond a monosyllable.

445
00:26:59,233 --> 00:27:03,832
Miss Darcy was tall, and on a larger scale than Elizabeth; and, though little more than

446
00:27:03,832 --> 00:27:08,532
sixteen, her figure was formed, and her appearance womanly and graceful.

447
00:27:08,532 --> 00:27:13,032
She was less handsome than her brother, but there was sense and good-humour in her face,

448
00:27:13,032 --> 00:27:15,799
and her manners were perfectly unassuming and gentle.

449
00:27:15,799 --> 00:27:20,532
Elizabeth, who had expected to find in her as acute and unembarrassed an observer as

450
00:27:20,532 --> 00:27:25,799
ever Mr. Darcy had been, was much relieved by discerning such different feelings.

451
00:27:25,799 --> 00:27:30,099
They had not been long together before Darcy told her that Bingley was also coming to wait

452
00:27:30,099 --> 00:27:35,933
on her; and she had barely time to express her satisfaction, and prepare for such a visitor,

453
00:27:35,933 --> 00:27:41,099
when Bingley’s quick step was heard on the stairs, and in a moment he entered the room.

454
00:27:41,099 --> 00:27:46,200
All Elizabeth’s anger against him had been long done away; but had she still felt any,

455
00:27:46,200 --> 00:27:50,666
it could hardly have stood its ground against the unaffected cordiality with which he expressed

456
00:27:50,666 --> 00:27:52,700
himself on seeing her again.

457
00:27:52,700 --> 00:27:57,565
He inquired in a friendly, though general, way, after her family, and looked and spoke

458
00:27:57,565 --> 00:28:00,966
with the same good-humoured ease that he had ever done.

459
00:28:00,966 --> 00:28:05,966
To Mr. and Mrs. Gardiner he was scarcely a less interesting personage than to herself.

460
00:28:05,966 --> 00:28:07,966
They had long wished to see him.

461
00:28:07,966 --> 00:28:11,299
The whole party before them, indeed, excited a lively attention.

462
00:28:11,299 --> 00:28:15,733
The suspicions which had just arisen of Mr. Darcy and their niece, directed their observation

463
00:28:15,733 --> 00:28:20,666
towards each with an earnest, though guarded, inquiry; and they soon drew from those inquiries

464
00:28:20,666 --> 00:28:25,233
the full conviction that one of them at least knew what it was to love.

465
00:28:25,233 --> 00:28:29,265
Of the lady’s sensations they remained a little in doubt; but that the gentleman was

466
00:28:29,265 --> 00:28:32,332
overflowing with admiration was evident enough.

467
00:28:32,332 --> 00:28:35,200
Elizabeth, on her side, had much to do.

468
00:28:35,200 --> 00:28:39,532
She wanted to ascertain the feelings of each of her visitors, she wanted to compose her

469
00:28:39,532 --> 00:28:44,565
own, and to make herself agreeable to all; and in the latter object, where she feared

470
00:28:44,565 --> 00:28:49,233
most to fail, she was most sure of success, for those to whom she endeavoured to give

471
00:28:49,233 --> 00:28:52,233
pleasure were pre-possessed in her favour.

472
00:28:52,233 --> 00:28:56,666
Bingley was ready, Georgiana was eager, and Darcy determined, to be pleased.

473
00:28:58,666 --> 00:29:02,065
In seeing Bingley, her thoughts naturally flew to her sister; and oh!

474
00:29:02,065 --> 00:29:07,299
how ardently did she long to know whether any of his were directed in a like manner.

475
00:29:07,299 --> 00:29:11,599
Sometimes she could fancy that he talked less than on former occasions, and once or twice

476
00:29:11,599 --> 00:29:17,032
pleased herself with the notion that, as he looked at her, he was trying to trace a resemblance.

477
00:29:17,032 --> 00:29:21,900
But, though this might be imaginary, she could not be deceived as to his behaviour to Miss

478
00:29:21,900 --> 00:29:25,265
Darcy, who had been set up as a rival to Jane.

479
00:29:25,265 --> 00:29:29,332
No look appeared on either side that spoke particular regard.

480
00:29:29,332 --> 00:29:33,099
Nothing occurred between them that could justify the hopes of his sister.

481
00:29:33,099 --> 00:29:37,400
On this point she was soon satisfied; and two or three little circumstances occurred

482
00:29:37,400 --> 00:29:43,032
ere they parted, which, in her anxious interpretation, denoted a recollection of Jane, not untinctured

483
00:29:43,032 --> 00:29:48,500
by tenderness, and a wish of saying more that might lead to the mention of her, had he dared.

484
00:29:48,500 --> 00:29:52,532
He observed to her, at a moment when the others were talking together, and in a tone which

485
00:29:52,532 --> 00:29:56,832
had something of real regret, that it “was a very long time since he had had the pleasure

486
00:29:56,832 --> 00:30:02,032
of seeing her;” and, before she could reply, he added, “It is above eight months.

487
00:30:02,032 --> 00:30:08,466
We have not met since the 26th of November, when we were all dancing together at Netherfield.”

488
00:30:08,466 --> 00:30:13,000
Elizabeth was pleased to find his memory so exact; and he afterwards took occasion to

489
00:30:13,000 --> 00:30:18,832
ask her, when unattended to by any of the rest, whether all her sisters were at Longbourn.

490
00:30:18,832 --> 00:30:22,765
There was not much in the question, nor in the preceding remark; but there was a look

491
00:30:22,765 --> 00:30:25,532
and a manner which gave them meaning.

492
00:30:25,532 --> 00:30:30,032
It was not often that she could turn her eyes on Mr. Darcy himself; but whenever she did

493
00:30:30,032 --> 00:30:35,233
catch a glimpse she saw an expression of general complaisance, and in all that he said, she

494
00:30:35,233 --> 00:30:40,000
heard an accent so far removed from hauteur or disdain of his companions, as convinced

495
00:30:40,000 --> 00:30:44,099
her that the improvement of manners which she had yesterday witnessed, however temporary

496
00:30:44,099 --> 00:30:48,532
its existence might prove, had at least outlived one day.

497
00:30:48,532 --> 00:30:52,933
When she saw him thus seeking the acquaintance, and courting the good opinion of people with

498
00:30:52,933 --> 00:30:57,265
whom any intercourse a few months ago would have been a disgrace; when she saw him thus

499
00:30:57,265 --> 00:31:03,233
civil, not only to herself, but to the very relations whom he had openly disdained, and

500
00:31:03,233 --> 00:31:08,466
recollected their last lively scene in Hunsford Parsonage, the difference, the change was

501
00:31:08,466 --> 00:31:14,299
so great, and struck so forcibly on her mind, that she could hardly restrain her astonishment

502
00:31:14,299 --> 00:31:15,765
from being visible.

503
00:31:15,765 --> 00:31:20,666
Never, even in the company of his dear friends at Netherfield, or his dignified relations

504
00:31:20,666 --> 00:31:27,599
at Rosings, had she seen him so desirous to please, so free from self-consequence or unbending

505
00:31:27,599 --> 00:31:32,233
reserve, as now, when no importance could result from the success of his endeavours,

506
00:31:32,233 --> 00:31:36,065
and when even the acquaintance of those to whom his attentions were addressed, would

507
00:31:36,065 --> 00:31:41,065
draw down the ridicule and censure of the ladies both of Netherfield and Rosings.

508
00:31:41,065 --> 00:31:45,233
Their visitors stayed with them above half an hour; and when they arose to depart, Mr.

509
00:31:45,233 --> 00:31:49,433
Darcy called on his sister to join him in expressing their wish of seeing Mr. and Mrs.

510
00:31:49,433 --> 00:31:54,500
Gardiner, and Miss Bennet, to dinner at Pemberley, before they left the country.

511
00:31:54,500 --> 00:31:59,332
Miss Darcy, though with a diffidence which marked her little in the habit of giving invitations,

512
00:31:59,332 --> 00:32:00,700
readily obeyed.

513
00:32:00,700 --> 00:32:06,032
Mrs. Gardiner looked at her niece, desirous of knowing how she, whom the invitation most

514
00:32:06,032 --> 00:32:11,166
concerned, felt disposed as to its acceptance, but Elizabeth had turned away her head.

515
00:32:11,166 --> 00:32:15,565
Presuming, however, that this studied avoidance spoke rather a momentary embarrassment than

516
00:32:15,565 --> 00:32:21,099
any dislike of the proposal, and seeing in her husband, who was fond of society, a perfect

517
00:32:21,099 --> 00:32:25,966
willingness to accept it, she ventured to engage for her attendance, and the day after

518
00:32:25,966 --> 00:32:28,366
the next was fixed on.

519
00:32:28,366 --> 00:32:32,799
Bingley expressed great pleasure in the certainty of seeing Elizabeth again, having still a

520
00:32:32,799 --> 00:32:38,065
great deal to say to her, and many inquiries to make after all their Hertfordshire friends.

521
00:32:38,065 --> 00:32:43,265
Elizabeth, construing all this into a wish of hearing her speak of her sister, was pleased;

522
00:32:43,265 --> 00:32:48,233
and on this account, as well as some others, found herself, when their visitors left them,

523
00:32:48,233 --> 00:32:52,933
capable of considering the last half hour with some satisfaction, though while it was

524
00:32:52,933 --> 00:32:55,666
passing the enjoyment of it had been little.

525
00:32:55,666 --> 00:33:00,532
Eager to be alone, and fearful of inquiries or hints from her uncle and aunt, she stayed

526
00:33:00,532 --> 00:33:04,966
with them only long enough to hear their favourable opinion of Bingley, and then hurried away

527
00:33:04,966 --> 00:33:05,966
to dress.

528
00:33:05,966 --> 00:33:11,366
But she had no reason to fear Mr. and Mrs. Gardiner’s curiosity; it was not their wish

529
00:33:11,366 --> 00:33:13,466
to force her communication.

530
00:33:13,466 --> 00:33:18,433
It was evident that she was much better acquainted with Mr. Darcy than they had before any idea

531
00:33:18,433 --> 00:33:22,366
of; it was evident that he was very much in love with her.

532
00:33:22,366 --> 00:33:27,032
They saw much to interest, but nothing to justify inquiry.

533
00:33:27,032 --> 00:33:31,933
Of Mr. Darcy it was now a matter of anxiety to think well; and, as far as their acquaintance

534
00:33:31,933 --> 00:33:34,366
reached, there was no fault to find.

535
00:33:34,366 --> 00:33:38,733
They could not be untouched by his politeness; and had they drawn his character from their

536
00:33:38,733 --> 00:33:43,733
own feelings and his servant’s report, without any reference to any other account, the circle

537
00:33:43,733 --> 00:33:48,700
in Hertfordshire to which he was known would not have recognized it for Mr. Darcy.

538
00:33:48,700 --> 00:33:53,366
There was now an interest, however, in believing the housekeeper; and they soon became sensible

539
00:33:53,366 --> 00:33:57,765
that the authority of a servant, who had known him since he was four years old, and whose

540
00:33:57,765 --> 00:34:02,700
own manners indicated respectability, was not to be hastily rejected.

541
00:34:02,700 --> 00:34:06,966
Neither had anything occurred in the intelligence of their Lambton friends that could materially

542
00:34:06,966 --> 00:34:08,599
lessen its weight.

543
00:34:08,599 --> 00:34:14,133
They had nothing to accuse him of but pride; pride he probably had, and if not, it would

544
00:34:14,133 --> 00:34:18,332
certainly be imputed by the inhabitants of a small market town where the family did not

545
00:34:18,332 --> 00:34:19,332
visit.

546
00:34:19,332 --> 00:34:24,000
It was acknowledged, however, that he was a liberal man, and did much good among the

547
00:34:24,000 --> 00:34:25,000
poor.

548
00:34:25,000 --> 00:34:30,332
With respect to Wickham, the travellers soon found that he was not held there in much estimation;

549
00:34:30,332 --> 00:34:34,733
for though the chief of his concerns with the son of his patron were imperfectly understood,

550
00:34:34,733 --> 00:34:39,666
it was yet a well-known fact that, on his quitting Derbyshire, he had left many debts

551
00:34:39,666 --> 00:34:44,599
behind him, which Mr. Darcy afterwards discharged.

552
00:34:44,599 --> 00:34:49,966
As for Elizabeth, her thoughts were at Pemberley this evening more than the last; and the evening,

553
00:34:49,966 --> 00:34:54,366
though as it passed it seemed long, was not long enough to determine her feelings towards

554
00:34:54,366 --> 00:35:00,000
one in that mansion; and she lay awake two whole hours, endeavouring to make them out.

555
00:35:00,000 --> 00:35:01,799
She certainly did not hate him.

556
00:35:01,799 --> 00:35:07,166
No; hatred had vanished long ago, and she had almost as long been ashamed of ever feeling

557
00:35:07,166 --> 00:35:10,065
a dislike against him, that could be so called.

558
00:35:10,065 --> 00:35:15,200
The respect created by the conviction of his valuable qualities, though at first unwillingly

559
00:35:15,200 --> 00:35:20,366
admitted, had for some time ceased to be repugnant to her feelings; and it was now heightened

560
00:35:20,366 --> 00:35:25,366
into somewhat of a friendlier nature by the testimony so highly in his favour, and bringing

561
00:35:25,366 --> 00:35:30,200
forward his disposition in so amiable a light, which yesterday had produced.

562
00:35:30,200 --> 00:35:35,099
But above all, above respect and esteem, there was a motive within her of good-will which

563
00:35:35,099 --> 00:35:36,966
could not be overlooked.

564
00:35:36,966 --> 00:35:41,832
It was gratitude;—gratitude, not merely for having once loved her, but for loving

565
00:35:41,832 --> 00:35:46,832
her still well enough to forgive all the petulance and acrimony of her manner in rejecting him,

566
00:35:46,832 --> 00:35:50,900
and all the unjust accusations accompanying her rejection.

567
00:35:50,900 --> 00:35:55,433
He who, she had been persuaded, would avoid her as his greatest enemy, seemed, on this

568
00:35:55,433 --> 00:36:00,299
accidental meeting, most eager to preserve the acquaintance; and without any indelicate

569
00:36:00,299 --> 00:36:05,799
display of regard, or any peculiarity of manner, where their two selves only were concerned,

570
00:36:05,799 --> 00:36:10,799
was soliciting the good opinion of her friends, and bent on making her known to his sister.

571
00:36:10,799 --> 00:36:16,400
Such a change in a man of so much pride excited not only astonishment but gratitude—for

572
00:36:16,400 --> 00:36:22,366
to love, ardent love, it must be attributed; and, as such, its impression on her was of

573
00:36:22,366 --> 00:36:28,433
a sort to be encouraged, as by no means unpleasing, though it could not be exactly defined.

574
00:36:28,433 --> 00:36:34,532
She respected, she esteemed, she was grateful to him, she felt a real interest in his welfare;

575
00:36:34,532 --> 00:36:39,200
and she only wanted to know how far she wished that welfare to depend upon herself, and how

576
00:36:39,200 --> 00:36:43,032
far it would be for the happiness of both that she should employ the power, which her

577
00:36:43,032 --> 00:36:48,065
fancy told her she still possessed, of bringing on the renewal of his addresses.

578
00:36:48,065 --> 00:36:52,433
It had been settled in the evening, between the aunt and niece, that such a striking civility

579
00:36:52,433 --> 00:36:56,832
as Miss Darcy’s, in coming to them on the very day of her arrival at Pemberley—for

580
00:36:56,832 --> 00:37:02,433
she had reached it only to a late breakfast—ought to be imitated, though it could not be equalled,

581
00:37:02,433 --> 00:37:07,166
by some exertion of politeness on their side; and, consequently, that it would be highly

582
00:37:07,166 --> 00:37:10,700
expedient to wait on her at Pemberley the following morning.

583
00:37:10,700 --> 00:37:12,765
They were, therefore, to go.

584
00:37:12,765 --> 00:37:16,666
Elizabeth was pleased; though when she asked herself the reason, she had very little to

585
00:37:16,666 --> 00:37:18,532
say in reply.

586
00:37:18,532 --> 00:37:21,200
Mr. Gardiner left them soon after breakfast.

587
00:37:21,200 --> 00:37:25,433
The fishing scheme had been renewed the day before, and a positive engagement made of

588
00:37:25,433 --> 00:37:28,500
his meeting some of the gentlemen at Pemberley by noon.

589
00:37:36,466 --> 00:37:39,466
CHAPTER XLV.

590
00:37:39,466 --> 00:37:44,099
CONVINCED as Elizabeth now was that Miss Bingley’s dislike of her had originated in jealousy,

591
00:37:44,099 --> 00:37:48,466
she could not help feeling how very unwelcome her appearance at Pemberley must be to her,

592
00:37:48,466 --> 00:37:52,565
and was curious to know with how much civility on that lady’s side the acquaintance would

593
00:37:52,565 --> 00:37:54,532
now be renewed.

594
00:37:54,532 --> 00:37:58,966
On reaching the house, they were shown through the hall into the saloon, whose northern aspect

595
00:37:58,966 --> 00:38:01,233
rendered it delightful for summer.

596
00:38:01,233 --> 00:38:05,733
Its windows, opening to the ground, admitted a most refreshing view of the high woody hills

597
00:38:05,733 --> 00:38:10,400
behind the house, and of the beautiful oaks and Spanish chestnuts which were scattered

598
00:38:10,400 --> 00:38:12,765
over the intermediate lawn.

599
00:38:12,765 --> 00:38:16,866
In this room they were received by Miss Darcy, who was sitting there with Mrs. Hurst and

600
00:38:16,866 --> 00:38:20,400
Miss Bingley, and the lady with whom she lived in London.

601
00:38:20,400 --> 00:38:25,565
Georgiana’s reception of them was very civil, but attended with all that embarrassment which,

602
00:38:25,565 --> 00:38:29,565
though proceeding from shyness and the fear of doing wrong, would easily give to those

603
00:38:29,565 --> 00:38:34,332
who felt themselves inferior the belief of her being proud and reserved.

604
00:38:34,332 --> 00:38:38,532
Mrs. Gardiner and her niece, however, did her justice, and pitied her.

605
00:38:38,532 --> 00:38:43,799
By Mrs. Hurst and Miss Bingley they were noticed only by a courtesy; and on their being seated,

606
00:38:43,799 --> 00:38:49,700
a pause, awkward as such pauses must always be, succeeded for a few moments.

607
00:38:49,700 --> 00:38:54,733
It was first broken by Mrs. Annesley, a genteel, agreeable-looking woman, whose endeavour to

608
00:38:54,733 --> 00:38:59,032
introduce some kind of discourse proved her to be more truly well-bred than either of

609
00:38:59,032 --> 00:39:04,765
the others; and between her and Mrs. Gardiner, with occasional help from Elizabeth, the conversation

610
00:39:04,765 --> 00:39:06,366
was carried on.

611
00:39:06,366 --> 00:39:11,099
Miss Darcy looked as if she wished for courage enough to join in it; and sometimes did venture

612
00:39:11,099 --> 00:39:15,866
a short sentence, when there was least danger of its being heard.

613
00:39:15,866 --> 00:39:20,133
Elizabeth soon saw that she was herself closely watched by Miss Bingley, and that she could

614
00:39:20,133 --> 00:39:25,265
not speak a word, especially to Miss Darcy, without calling her attention.

615
00:39:25,265 --> 00:39:29,099
This observation would not have prevented her from trying to talk to the latter, had

616
00:39:29,099 --> 00:39:33,900
they not been seated at an inconvenient distance; but she was not sorry to be spared the necessity

617
00:39:33,900 --> 00:39:37,299
of saying much: her own thoughts were employing her.

618
00:39:37,299 --> 00:39:41,700
She expected every moment that some of the gentlemen would enter the room: she wished,

619
00:39:41,700 --> 00:39:45,966
she feared, that the master of the house might be amongst them; and whether she wished or

620
00:39:45,966 --> 00:39:49,233
feared it most, she could scarcely determine.

621
00:39:49,233 --> 00:39:52,666
After sitting in this manner a quarter of an hour, without hearing Miss Bingley’s

622
00:39:52,666 --> 00:39:57,000
voice, Elizabeth was roused by receiving from her a cold inquiry after the health of her

623
00:39:57,000 --> 00:39:58,000
family.

624
00:39:58,000 --> 00:40:02,433
She answered with equal indifference and brevity, and the other said no more.

625
00:40:02,433 --> 00:40:06,700
The next variation which their visit afforded was produced by the entrance of servants with

626
00:40:06,700 --> 00:40:12,332
cold meat, cake, and a variety of all the finest fruits in season; but this did not

627
00:40:12,332 --> 00:40:17,433
take place till after many a significant look and smile from Mrs. Annesley to Miss Darcy

628
00:40:17,433 --> 00:40:20,400
had been given, to remind her of her post.

629
00:40:20,400 --> 00:40:24,866
There was now employment for the whole party; for though they could not all talk, they could

630
00:40:24,866 --> 00:40:30,000
all eat; and the beautiful pyramids of grapes, nectarines, and peaches, soon collected them

631
00:40:30,000 --> 00:40:31,733
round the table.

632
00:40:31,733 --> 00:40:36,933
While thus engaged, Elizabeth had a fair opportunity of deciding whether she most feared or wished

633
00:40:36,933 --> 00:40:41,966
for the appearance of Mr. Darcy, by the feelings which prevailed on his entering the room;

634
00:40:41,966 --> 00:40:46,299
and then, though but a moment before she had believed her wishes to predominate, she began

635
00:40:46,299 --> 00:40:48,733
to regret that he came.

636
00:40:48,733 --> 00:40:52,866
He had been some time with Mr. Gardiner, who, with two or three other gentlemen from the

637
00:40:52,866 --> 00:40:57,133
house, was engaged by the river; and had left him only on learning that the ladies of the

638
00:40:57,133 --> 00:41:00,433
family intended a visit to Georgiana that morning.

639
00:41:00,433 --> 00:41:05,633
No sooner did he appear, than Elizabeth wisely resolved to be perfectly easy and unembarrassed;—a

640
00:41:05,633 --> 00:41:10,666
resolution the more necessary to be made, but perhaps not the more easily kept, because

641
00:41:10,666 --> 00:41:14,700
she saw that the suspicions of the whole party were awakened against them, and that there

642
00:41:14,700 --> 00:41:19,233
was scarcely an eye which did not watch his behaviour when he first came into the room.

643
00:41:19,233 --> 00:41:24,032
In no countenance was attentive curiosity so strongly marked as in Miss Bingley’s,

644
00:41:24,032 --> 00:41:28,565
in spite of the smiles which overspread her face whenever she spoke to one of its objects;

645
00:41:28,565 --> 00:41:33,000
for jealousy had not yet made her desperate, and her attentions to Mr. Darcy were by no

646
00:41:33,000 --> 00:41:34,599
means over.

647
00:41:34,599 --> 00:41:39,466
Miss Darcy, on her brother’s entrance, exerted herself much more to talk; and Elizabeth saw

648
00:41:39,466 --> 00:41:43,799
that he was anxious for his sister and herself to get acquainted, and forwarded, as much

649
00:41:43,799 --> 00:41:47,733
as possible, every attempt at conversation on either side.

650
00:41:47,733 --> 00:41:52,599
Miss Bingley saw all this likewise; and, in the imprudence of anger, took the first opportunity

651
00:41:52,599 --> 00:41:58,099
of saying, with sneering civility,— “Pray, Miss Eliza, are not the ——shire

652
00:41:58,099 --> 00:42:00,299
militia removed from Meryton?

653
00:42:00,299 --> 00:42:04,633
They must be a great loss to your family.”

654
00:42:04,633 --> 00:42:08,832
In Darcy’s presence she dared not mention Wickham’s name: but Elizabeth instantly

655
00:42:08,832 --> 00:42:13,565
comprehended that he was uppermost in her thoughts; and the various recollections connected

656
00:42:13,565 --> 00:42:18,532
with him gave her a moment’s distress; but, exerting herself vigorously to repel the ill-natured

657
00:42:18,532 --> 00:42:23,332
attack, she presently answered the question in a tolerably disengaged tone.

658
00:42:23,332 --> 00:42:28,666
While she spoke, an involuntary glance showed her Darcy with a heightened complexion, earnestly

659
00:42:28,666 --> 00:42:34,133
looking at her, and his sister overcome with confusion, and unable to lift up her eyes.

660
00:42:34,133 --> 00:42:38,400
Had Miss Bingley known what pain she was then giving her beloved friend, she undoubtedly

661
00:42:38,400 --> 00:42:42,733
would have refrained from the hint; but she had merely intended to discompose Elizabeth,

662
00:42:42,733 --> 00:42:47,599
by bringing forward the idea of a man to whom she believed her partial, to make her betray

663
00:42:47,599 --> 00:42:52,433
a sensibility which might injure her in Darcy’s opinion, and, perhaps, to remind the latter

664
00:42:52,433 --> 00:42:56,332
of all the follies and absurdities by which some part of her family were connected with

665
00:42:56,332 --> 00:42:57,799
that corps.

666
00:42:57,799 --> 00:43:01,400
Not a syllable had ever reached her of Miss Darcy’s meditated elopement.

667
00:43:01,400 --> 00:43:06,532
To no creature had it been revealed, where secrecy was possible, except to Elizabeth;

668
00:43:06,532 --> 00:43:11,299
and from all Bingley’s connections her brother was particularly anxious to conceal it, from

669
00:43:11,299 --> 00:43:15,633
that very wish which Elizabeth had long ago attributed to him, of their becoming hereafter

670
00:43:15,633 --> 00:43:16,765
her own.

671
00:43:16,765 --> 00:43:20,700
He had certainly formed such a plan; and without meaning that it should affect his endeavour

672
00:43:20,700 --> 00:43:25,233
to separate him from Miss Bennet, it is probable that it might add something to his lively

673
00:43:25,233 --> 00:43:27,933
concern for the welfare of his friend.

674
00:43:27,933 --> 00:43:32,866
Elizabeth’s collected behaviour, however, soon quieted his emotion; and as Miss Bingley,

675
00:43:32,866 --> 00:43:37,799
vexed and disappointed, dared not approach nearer to Wickham, Georgiana also recovered

676
00:43:37,799 --> 00:43:41,400
in time, though not enough to be able to speak any more.

677
00:43:41,400 --> 00:43:46,133
Her brother, whose eye she feared to meet, scarcely recollected her interest in the affair;

678
00:43:46,133 --> 00:43:49,966
and the very circumstance which had been designed to turn his thoughts from Elizabeth, seemed

679
00:43:49,966 --> 00:43:54,032
to have fixed them on her more and more cheerfully.

680
00:43:54,032 --> 00:43:58,400
Their visit did not continue long after the question and answer above mentioned; and while

681
00:43:58,400 --> 00:44:03,200
Mr. Darcy was attending them to their carriage, Miss Bingley was venting her feelings in criticisms

682
00:44:03,200 --> 00:44:06,400
on Elizabeth’s person, behaviour, and dress.

683
00:44:06,400 --> 00:44:08,366
But Georgiana would not join her.

684
00:44:08,366 --> 00:44:12,433
Her brother’s recommendation was enough to insure her favour: his judgment could not

685
00:44:12,433 --> 00:44:17,933
err; and he had spoken in such terms of Elizabeth, as to leave Georgiana without the power of

686
00:44:17,933 --> 00:44:21,265
finding her otherwise than lovely and amiable.

687
00:44:21,265 --> 00:44:25,466
When Darcy returned to the saloon, Miss Bingley could not help repeating to him some part

688
00:44:25,466 --> 00:44:27,933
of what she had been saying to his sister.

689
00:44:27,933 --> 00:44:33,765
“How very ill Eliza Bennet looks this morning, Mr. Darcy,” she cried: “I never in my

690
00:44:33,765 --> 00:44:37,466
life saw anyone so much altered as she is since the winter.

691
00:44:37,466 --> 00:44:39,866
She is grown so brown and coarse!

692
00:44:39,866 --> 00:44:44,332
Louisa and I were agreeing that we should not have known her again.”

693
00:44:44,332 --> 00:44:48,133
However little Mr. Darcy might have liked such an address, he contented himself with

694
00:44:48,133 --> 00:44:52,733
coolly replying, that he perceived no other alteration than her being rather tanned,—no

695
00:44:52,733 --> 00:44:55,400
miraculous consequence of travelling in the summer.

696
00:44:55,400 --> 00:45:00,733
“For my own part,” she rejoined, “I must confess that I never could see any beauty

697
00:45:00,733 --> 00:45:01,733
in her.

698
00:45:01,733 --> 00:45:07,233
Her face is too thin; her complexion has no brilliancy; and her features are not at all

699
00:45:07,233 --> 00:45:08,233
handsome.

700
00:45:08,233 --> 00:45:11,500
Her nose wants character; there is nothing marked in its lines.

701
00:45:11,500 --> 00:45:18,166
Her teeth are tolerable, but not out of the common way; and as for her eyes, which have

702
00:45:18,166 --> 00:45:23,032
sometimes been called so fine, I never could perceive anything extraordinary in them.

703
00:45:23,032 --> 00:45:28,233
They have a sharp, shrewish look, which I do not like at all; and in her air altogether,

704
00:45:28,233 --> 00:45:33,433
there is a self-sufficiency without fashion, which is intolerable.”

705
00:45:33,433 --> 00:45:38,200
Persuaded as Miss Bingley was that Darcy admired Elizabeth, this was not the best method of

706
00:45:38,200 --> 00:45:42,733
recommending herself; but angry people are not always wise; and in seeing him at last

707
00:45:42,733 --> 00:45:46,832
look somewhat nettled, she had all the success she expected.

708
00:45:46,832 --> 00:45:51,900
He was resolutely silent, however; and, from a determination of making him speak, she continued,—

709
00:45:51,900 --> 00:45:56,933
“I remember, when we first knew her in Hertfordshire, how amazed we all were to find that she was

710
00:45:56,933 --> 00:46:02,099
a reputed beauty; and I particularly recollect your saying one night, after they had been

711
00:46:02,099 --> 00:46:04,400
dining at Netherfield, ‘She a beauty!

712
00:46:04,400 --> 00:46:08,332
I should as soon call her mother a wit.’

713
00:46:08,332 --> 00:46:12,532
But afterwards she seemed to improve on you, and I believe you thought her rather pretty

714
00:46:12,532 --> 00:46:13,532
at one time.”

715
00:46:13,532 --> 00:46:19,233
“Yes,” replied Darcy, who could contain himself no longer, “but that was only when

716
00:46:19,233 --> 00:46:23,700
I first knew her; for it is many months since I have considered her as one of the handsomest

717
00:46:23,700 --> 00:46:26,433
women of my acquaintance.”

718
00:46:26,433 --> 00:46:30,666
He then went away, and Miss Bingley was left to all the satisfaction of having forced him

719
00:46:30,666 --> 00:46:34,400
to say what gave no one any pain but herself.

720
00:46:34,400 --> 00:46:38,433
Mrs. Gardiner and Elizabeth talked of all that had occurred during their visit, as they

721
00:46:38,433 --> 00:46:42,166
returned, except what had particularly interested them both.

722
00:46:42,166 --> 00:46:46,400
The looks and behaviour of everybody they had seen were discussed, except of the person

723
00:46:46,400 --> 00:46:48,733
who had mostly engaged their attention.

724
00:46:48,733 --> 00:46:54,765
They talked of his sister, his friends, his house, his fruit, of everything but himself;

725
00:46:54,765 --> 00:46:58,900
yet Elizabeth was longing to know what Mrs. Gardiner thought of him, and Mrs. Gardiner

726
00:46:58,900 --> 00:47:10,000
would have been highly gratified by her niece’s beginning the subject.
